    "durationMs": 178202,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL shortener is complete and fully verified. Summary of what I built in `/workspace`:\n\n**Stack**: Express 4.21.2 (exact), Knex 3 + `pg` as the persistence layer, Node 22, PostgreSQL via `DATABASE_URL` (nothing hard-coded).\n\n**Files**: `src/app.js` (routes/validation), `src/server.js`, `src/db.js` (knex instance), `src/migrate.js`, `migrations/20241121000000_create_links.js` (`links`: id, unique code, url, clicks, created_at), `start.sh`, `README.md`.\n\n**Verified by HTTP tests**:\n- POST /links → 201 with generated or custom code, URL returned unchanged (incl. port/query/fragment); duplicate custom code → 409\n- 422 for malformed JSON, missing/null/non-string url, `not-a-url`, `ftp://`, `http://`, `http:///path`, short/bad-char/non-string codes; 4–20 char codes accepted at both boundaries\n- GET /links → `{\"items\": [...], \"total\": n}` in ascending creation order; GET /links/{code} → 200/404\n- GET /r/{code} → 302 with exact `Location`, unknown → 404; 30 concurrent redirects produced exactly 30 clicks (atomic `clicks + 1`)\n- Persistence: killed the server, restarted via `sh start.sh` — links and click counts survived; migrations are idempotent (`knex_migrations` tracking)\n\nOne bug I caught during testing: Node's WHATWG URL parser accepts `http:///path` by absorbing the extra slash into the host, so I added a structural `^https?://` + non-empty-authority check on top of the parse to reject it like a conventional validator would.\n\nTest data was wiped and the test server stopped — `sh start.sh` will boot it fresh for the grading run."
